//? Title: k-Nearest Neighbours: Classification and Regression by Proximity
//? Subtitle: The k-NN rule, distances, standardisation, voting and exact leave-one-out evaluation, checked by hand, against the Cover–Hart asymptotics and the bias–variance decomposition, and applied to iris and wine, using datamunge's stats::KNNClassifier and stats::KNNRegressor
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given labelled training points, we want to predict the label of a new point without assuming
any form for the relation between predictors and label. The \(k\)-nearest-neighbour rule lets the
\(k\) closest training points vote (classification) or averages them (regression). Its behaviour is
decided by the distance, the scaling of the predictors, the voting scheme and \(k\). We derive the
rule, exact leave-one-out (LOO) evaluation with the standardisation recomputed for every left-out
row, the Cover–Hart large-sample risk and the bias–variance decomposition of k-NN regression, and
check each against datamunge's `stats::KNNClassifier` and `stats::KNNRegressor`. On six points the
predictions for every metric, \(k\) and weighting match the hand calculation, including the tie
rules. On two Gaussian classes the test error of 1-, 5- and 25-NN settles near the predicted
limits 0.225, 0.180 and 0.163 (Bayes risk 0.159), and LOO error tracks test error. Adding 128
noise predictors raises the 15-NN error from 0.165 to 0.381. The simulated mean squared error of
k-NN regression matches the exact bias-plus-variance prediction at every \(k\), with its minimum
at \(k=18\). On iris, LOO accuracy reaches 96.7% from the petal measurements alone; on wine,
standardising the 13 predictors raises LOO accuracy from 70–77% to 95–98%. The LOO accuracy paths
agree exactly with scikit-learn refitted with each row left out (checked for \(k\le25\) on all four measurements).
)

\toc

> The problem

We have \(n\) training points \(x_1,\dots,x_n\in\mathbb R^p\) with labels \(y_1,\dots,y_n\): a class out of a
finite set, or a number. We want to predict the label of a new point \(x\) without assuming any
form for the relation between \(x\) and \(y\): no linear boundary, no Gaussian classes, no
polynomial trend. The only assumption is smoothness: points that are close in \(\mathbb R^p\) tend to
have similar labels. The \(k\)-nearest-neighbour (k-NN) rule turns that assumption directly into
a prediction. It finds the \(k\) training points closest to \(x\) and lets them vote (for a class)
or averages them (for a number).

The difficulty is that everything rests on "close". Distance depends on the units of each
predictor, on predictors that carry no information, and on the dimension \(p\). The number of
neighbours \(k\) trades a jagged, high-variance rule (\(k=1\)) against an over-smoothed, biased one
(\(k\) large). There is also no training error to report, since with \(k=1\) every training point
predicts itself perfectly.

> The method

>> The rule

Let \(d\) be a distance on \(\mathbb R^p\) and let \(N_k(x)\) be the indices of the \(k\) training points
nearest to \(x\). For a class label, the *vote share* of class \(c\) is

$$
\hat p_c(x)=\frac{\sum_{i\in N_k(x)}w_i\,[y_i=c]}{\sum_{i\in N_k(x)}w_i},\qquad \hat y(x)=\arg\max_c\hat p_c(x),
$$
\alttext(The vote share p hat c of x is the sum over the k nearest neighbours of w i times the indicator that y i equals c, divided by the sum of the weights. The predicted class is the class with the largest share.)

and for a numeric label the prediction is the weighted mean
\(\hat f(x)=\sum_{i\in N_k(x)}w_iy_i\big/\sum_{i\in N_k(x)}w_i\). With *uniform* weights \(w_i=1\) every
neighbour counts equally; with *inverse-distance* weights \(w_i=1/d(x,x_i)\) closer neighbours count
more, and a training point at distance zero outvotes all others.

Two kinds of tie need a rule. Neighbours at equal distance are ordered by their training-row
position, and a tied vote goes to the first class in sorted order. Both conventions are
scikit-learn's, so datamunge's predictions can be compared with it one for one.

>> Distances

`DistanceMetric::Euclidean` is \(\lVert a-b\rVert_2=\sqrt{\sum_j(a_j-b_j)^2}\) and
`DistanceMetric::Manhattan` is \(\lVert a-b\rVert_1=\sum_j|a_j-b_j|\). Their unit balls are a disc and a
diamond. Euclidean distance lets one large difference dominate, while Manhattan distance adds the
coordinates' differences as they are, so the two can rank neighbours differently: from the
origin, \((2,0)\) and \((1.2,1.2)\) are at Euclidean distances 2 and 1.70, but at Manhattan distances 2
and 2.4.

>> Standardisation

A distance adds up differences in different units. If one predictor is measured in grams and
another in kilograms, the first one decides the ranking almost alone. The usual remedy is to
rescale each predictor to mean 0 and standard deviation 1,
\(z_{ij}=(x_{ij}-\bar x_j)/s_j\), with \(s_j\) the standard deviation of predictor \(j\) (divisor \(n\),
as scikit-learn's `StandardScaler`). Then each predictor has the same spread. This is the
default (`standardize = true`). datamunge computes each standardised difference as the raw
difference times \(1/s_j\), so points that are exactly tied in the raw data stay exactly tied. It is a modelling choice, not a law: it gives a noise predictor
the same weight as an informative one.

>> Leave-one-out evaluation

The training error of k-NN is meaningless: with \(k=1\) each training point is its own nearest
neighbour and is classified correctly. `KNNClassifier` therefore reports *leave-one-out* (LOO)
results: row \(i\) is classified by the other \(n-1\) rows. The standardisation is also recomputed
without row \(i\), so the result equals refitting the model \(n\) times. Removing \(x_i\) changes only the
scales (the means cancel in differences), and the centred sum of squares downdates as

$$
SS_{-i,j}=SS_j-\frac{n}{n-1}\,(x_{ij}-\bar x_j)^2,\qquad s_{-i,j}=\sqrt{SS_{-i,j}/(n-1)},
$$
\alttext(The sum of squares without row i equals the full sum of squares minus n over n minus 1 times the squared deviation of x i j from the mean. The standard deviation without row i is the square root of that divided by n minus 1.)

so each standardised difference is multiplied by \(s_j/s_{-i,j}\) and no refit is needed.
Because sorting the other rows by distance is the expensive part, `loo_accuracy_path(K)`
sorts once per row and returns the LOO accuracy for every \(k\le K\) at the cost of a single
evaluation (`loo_rmse_path` for the regressor).

>> What k-NN estimates: the Cover–Hart limit

For two classes write \(\eta(x)=P(Y=1\mid X=x)\). The best possible rule predicts class 1 when
\(\eta(x)>\tfrac12\) and has the *Bayes risk* \(R^*=E[\min(\eta,1-\eta)]\). As \(n\to\infty\) with \(k\) fixed, the
\(k\) neighbours of \(x\) crowd in on \(x\), so their labels become independent Bernoulli(\(\eta(x)\)) draws.
With \(B\sim\text{Bin}(k,\eta)\) votes for class 1, the rule errs with conditional probability

$$
r_k(\eta)=\eta\,P(B<k/2)+(1-\eta)\,P(B>k/2)+\tfrac12P(B=k/2),\qquad R_k=E\big[r_k(\eta(X))\big],
$$
\alttext(r k of eta equals eta times the probability that B is less than k over 2, plus 1 minus eta times the probability that B is more than k over 2, plus one half times the probability of a tie. The asymptotic risk R k is the expectation of r k of eta of X.)

which `stats::knn_asymptotic_risk(eta, k)` evaluates. For \(k=1\), \(r_1=2\eta(1-\eta)\). Since
\(\min(\eta,1-\eta)\le2\eta(1-\eta)\le2\min(\eta,1-\eta)\), the 1-NN rule is never worse than twice Bayes; the
sharper bound of Cover and Hart (1967) is \(R^*\le R_1\le2R^*(1-R^*)\). As \(k\) grows, \(r_k(\eta)\to\min(\eta,1-\eta)\)
and \(R_k\to R^*\). In practice \(k\) must grow more slowly than \(n\) so the neighbours stay local
(\(k\to\infty\), \(k/n\to0\)); then k-NN is *universally consistent* (Stone, 1977).

>> Regression: bias and variance

For \(y_i=f(x_i)+\varepsilon_i\) with independent noise of variance \(\sigma^2\), and with the design points held
fixed, the uniform k-NN estimate at \(x_0\) has

$$
E\,\hat f(x_0)=\frac1k\sum_{i\in N_k(x_0)}f(x_i),\qquad \operatorname{Var}\hat f(x_0)=\frac{\sigma^2}{k},
$$
\alttext(The expected k-NN estimate at x 0 is the mean of f over the k neighbours. Its variance is sigma squared over k.)

because the neighbour set depends on the \(x\)'s only. The variance falls as \(1/k\) while the bias
\(E\hat f(x_0)-f(x_0)\) grows as the neighbours spread out, so the mean squared error is U-shaped
in \(k\) (Hastie, Tibshirani and Friedman, 2009, section 7.3).

>> The curse of dimensionality

In \(p\) dimensions a neighbourhood holding a fixed fraction \(r\) of uniformly spread data must
span a fraction \(r^{1/p}\) of each axis: 10% of the data in 10 dimensions needs 79% of each
range. Neighbours stop being local. Distances also concentrate: for independent coordinates
the distances from a query to all training points have mean of order \(\sqrt p\) and a spread
of order 1, so the nearest and farthest points differ by a vanishing fraction. Every
uninformative predictor adds noise to every distance.

> Simple examples

>> Six points by hand

Six training points in the plane, three of class *a* and three of class *b*, and a query at the
origin. The predictors are not standardised, so the distances can be checked by hand.

| row | class | \(x_1\) | \(x_2\) | Euclidean | Manhattan |
| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | b | 1.0 | 0.5 | \(\sqrt{1.25}=1.118\) | 1.5 |
| 1 | a | −1.5 | 0.0 | 1.5 | 1.5 |
| 2 | a | 0.0 | −1.6 | 1.6 | 1.6 |
| 3 | b | 1.2 | 1.2 | \(\sqrt{2.88}=1.697\) | 2.4 |
| 4 | a | −1.3 | −1.3 | \(\sqrt{3.38}=1.838\) | 2.6 |
| 5 | b | 2.0 | 0.2 | \(\sqrt{4.04}=2.010\) | 2.2 |

In Euclidean distance the order is rows 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 (classes b, a, a, b, a, b). So \(k=1\)
predicts *b*, \(k=3\) predicts *a* (2 votes to 1), and \(k=5\) predicts *a* (3 to 2). In Manhattan
distance rows 0 and 1 tie at 1.5, and row order puts row 0 first. The order is rows 0, 1, 2, 5,
3, 4 (b, a, a, b, b, a), so \(k=1\) predicts *b* (through the tie rule), \(k=3\) predicts *a*, and
\(k=5\) predicts *b* (3 to 2). With inverse-distance weights and \(k=5\), Euclidean class *a* gets
\(1/1.5+1/1.6+1/1.838=1.836\) against *b*'s \(1/1.118+1/1.697=1.484\): a share of 0.553 for *a*. In
Manhattan distance *b* gets \(1/1.5+1/2.2+1/2.4=1.538\) against *a*'s \(1/1.5+1/1.6=1.292\): a share
of 0.456 for *a*.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/knn_hand.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;
using dm::stats::DistanceMetric;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  DF train;
  train.add_column("x1", std::vector<double>{1.0, -1.5, 0.0, 1.2, -1.3, 2.0});
  train.add_column("x2", std::vector<double>{0.5, 0.0, -1.6, 1.2, -1.3, 0.2});
  train.add_column("class", std::vector<std::string>{"b", "a", "a", "b", "a", "b"});
  DF query;
  query.add_column("x1", std::vector<double>{0.0});
  query.add_column("x2", std::vector<double>{0.0});

  std::vector<std::string> metric, voting, order;
  std::vector<double> ks, share_a;
  std::vector<std::string> predicted;
  for (const auto m : {DistanceMetric::Euclidean, DistanceMetric::Manhattan})
    for (const bool weighted : {false, true})
      for (const std::size_t k : {1u, 3u, 5u}) {
        const dm::stats::KNNClassifier knn(train, "class ~ x1 + x2",
                                           {.k = k, .metric = m, .weighted = weighted, .standardize = false});
        const auto detail = knn.predict_detail(query);
        metric.push_back(m == DistanceMetric::Euclidean ? "Euclidean" : "Manhattan");
        voting.push_back(weighted ? "1/d" : "uniform");
        ks.push_back(double(k));
        order.push_back(std::format("{}", knn.kneighbors(query).index[0]));
        share_a.push_back(detail.vote_share[0][0]);
        predicted.push_back(detail.class_label[0]);
      }
  DF table;
  table.add_column("metric", metric);
  table.add_column("weights", voting);
  table.add_column("k", ks);
  table.add_column("neighbours", order);
  table.add_column("share of a", share_a);
  table.add_column("prediction", predicted);
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 3;
  md.column_digits = {{"k", 0}};
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(md));

  // Left: the points, the query and the k = 3 neighbourhoods in both metrics.
  const auto blue = dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, red = dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, grey = dm::RGB{100, 116, 139};
  auto geo = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> cx, cy, mx, my;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 200; ++i) {
    const double t = 2.0 * std::numbers::pi * i / 200.0;
    cx.push_back(1.6 * std::cos(t)), cy.push_back(1.6 * std::sin(t));
  }
  geo.lines(cx, cy, "Euclidean, r = 1.6", grey, 1.5);
  geo.lines({1.6, 0.0, -1.6, 0.0, 1.6}, {0.0, 1.6, 0.0, -1.6, 0.0}, "Manhattan, r = 1.6", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 1.5);
  const auto& x1 = train.get_column<double>("x1").values();
  const auto& x2 = train.get_column<double>("x2").values();
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 6; ++i) {
    const bool is_b = i == 0 || i == 3 || i == 5;
    geo.points({x1[i]}, {x2[i]}, is_b ? red : blue, 7.0);
    geo.text(x1[i] + 0.22, x2[i] + 0.22, std::format("{}", i), dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 13.0);
  }
  geo.points({0.0}, {0.0}, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 7.0).text(0.0, -0.3, "query", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 13.0);
  geo.x_limits(-2.3, 2.7).y_limits(-2.2, 2.6).title("Neighbourhoods holding k = 3").x_label("x1").y_label("x2");

  // Right: the 1-NN decision regions (Euclidean), the Voronoi cells of the training points.
  const dm::stats::KNNClassifier one(train, "class ~ x1 + x2", {.k = 1, .standardize = false});
  auto regions = one.plot_decision_regions("x1", "x2", 200, std::array{-1.8, 2.3, -1.9, 2.3});
  regions.points({0.0}, {0.0}, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 7.0);
  regions.title("1-NN regions (Euclidean)");

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(geo).add(regions).size(1100, 480).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/knn_hand.svg");
}
```
