\image(figures/knn_hand.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the six training points (blue *a*, red *b*, labelled by row), the query at the origin, and the Euclidean disc and Manhattan diamond of radius 1.6, each just reaching the third neighbour, row 2 at \((0,-1.6)\). Right: the 1-NN regions in Euclidean distance; each training point owns the cell of the plane closer to it than to any other point, and the query (black) lies in row 0's cell. Table: the neighbours (nearest first) and the share of the votes for *a*.)
\alttext(Left: six points labelled 0 to 5, red for class b at (1, 0.5), (1.2, 1.2) and (2, 0.2), blue for class a at (-1.5, 0), (0, -1.6) and (-1.3, -1.3), and a black query point at the origin. A grey circle and an orange diamond of radius 1.6 centred on the query both pass through point 2 and enclose points 0 and 1. Right: the plane split into a blue region on the left and lower left and a pink region on the upper right, with a boundary made of straight segments; the query point lies just inside the pink region.)

The table reproduces the hand calculation, including both tie rules. Manhattan's \(k=1\)
prediction is *b* only because row 0 comes before row 1. The disc and the diamond of radius 1.6
both hold exactly rows 0, 1 and 2, but they differ beyond: the fourth Euclidean neighbour is row 3,
the fourth Manhattan neighbour row 5, which is why the two metrics disagree at \(k=5\). The
1-NN regions are unions of Voronoi cells, so their boundaries are pieces of the perpendicular
bisectors between an *a* point and a *b* point. The query sits just on the *b* side of the bisector
of rows 0 and 1, consistent with \(1.118<1.5\).

>> Regression on a line, and leave-one-out by hand

Six points \(x=1,\dots,6\) with \(y=(2,4,3,7,6,9)\). With \(k=1\) the prediction is the \(y\) of the nearest
\(x\), a step function that jumps halfway between neighbouring \(x\)'s. With \(k=3\) it is the mean of
three. The leave-one-out prediction of each point uses only the other five. For \(k=1\), \(x=1\)'s
nearest other point is \(x=2\), so it is predicted as 4. \(x=2\) is equidistant from \(x=1\) and \(x=3\),
and row order picks \(x=1\), predicting 2. Continuing, the \(k=1\) leave-one-out predictions are
\((4,2,4,3,7,6)\), with errors \((-2,2,-1,4,-1,3)\), so the leave-one-out RMSE is
\(\sqrt{35/6}=2.415\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/knn_line.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  DF data;
  data.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6});
  data.add_column("y", std::vector<double>{2, 4, 3, 7, 6, 9});
  const dm::stats::KNNRegressor k1(data, "y ~ x", {.k = 1});
  const dm::stats::KNNRegressor k3(data, "y ~ x", {.k = 3});
  DF table = data;
  table.add_column("LOO k = 1", k1.fitted_values());
  table.add_column("LOO k = 3", k3.fitted_values());
  std::print("{}\n\n", table.to_md(3));
  const auto path = k1.loo_rmse_path(5);
  std::print("Leave-one-out RMSE for k = 1, ..., 5: {::.3f}\n\n", path);

  auto fits = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> grid;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 600; ++i) grid.push_back(0.5 + 6.0 * i / 600.0);
  DF g;
  g.add_column("x", grid);
  fits.lines(grid, k1.predict(g), "k = 1", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .lines(grid, k3.predict(g), "k = 3", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0);
  fits.points(data.get_column<double>("x").values(), data.get_column<double>("y").values(), dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 7.0);
  fits.x_limits(0.5, 6.5).y_limits(0.0, 12.0).title("k-NN regression fits").x_label("x").y_label("y");

  auto loo = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  loo.lines({1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, path, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0).points({1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, path, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 7.0);
  loo.x_limits(0.5, 5.5).y_limits(0.0, 3.0).title("Leave-one-out RMSE").x_label("k").y_label("RMSE");

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(fits).add(loo).size(1100, 440).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/knn_line.svg");
}
```
