| 8 | 0.207 | 2.598 |
| 32 | 0.284 | 0.901 |
| 128 | 0.381 | 0.381 |


| k | bias^2 | sigma^2 / k | theory MSE | simulated MSE |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.0001 | 0.0900 | 0.0901 | 0.0878 |
| 2 | 0.0001 | 0.0450 | 0.0451 | 0.0439 |
| 3 | 0.0001 | 0.0300 | 0.0301 | 0.0297 |
| 5 | 0.0001 | 0.0180 | 0.0181 | 0.0180 |
| 8 | 0.0002 | 0.0112 | 0.0114 | 0.0114 |
| 12 | 0.0004 | 0.0075 | 0.0079 | 0.0079 |
| 18 | 0.0017 | 0.0050 | 0.0067 | 0.0067 |
| 27 | 0.0077 | 0.0033 | 0.0111 | 0.0108 |
| 40 | 0.0299 | 0.0022 | 0.0322 | 0.0318 |

\image(figures/knn_theory.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: test error of 15-NN on the two-Gaussian problem against the number of added noise predictors, with the Bayes risk 0.159 (grey). Right: integrated squared error of uniform k-NN regression of \(\sin(2\pi x)\) on 100 equally spaced points with \(\sigma=0.3\), against \(k\): squared bias (red) and variance \(\sigma^2/k\) (blue) computed exactly from the neighbour sets, their sum (black) and the average over 400 simulated data sets (green). Tables: the same numbers, with the relative contrast of the distances.)
\alttext(Left: test error of 15-NN against log2 of 1 plus the number of noise predictors: 0.165, 0.179, 0.207, 0.284 and 0.381 at 0, 2, 8, 32 and 128 noise predictors, rising away from a grey line at the Bayes risk 0.159. Right: mean squared error against log2 k from 0 to about 5.3. The blue variance curve falls from 0.09 at k equals 1. The red squared-bias curve is near zero until about k equals 12, then rises to 0.03 at k equals 40. The black total has its minimum near log2 k equals 4.2. Green simulated points lie on the black curve.)

*Noise predictors.* With standardisation, each noise predictor has the same weight as the
informative one, so its random differences are added to every distance. With 8 noise
predictors the error has risen from 0.165 to 0.207, with 128 to 0.381, not far from guessing.
The relative contrast shows why: with the two informative predictors alone the farthest
training point is 65 times as far as the nearest, with 128 noise predictors only 1.38 times.
The nearest neighbour is then hardly nearer than any other point.

*Bias and variance.* The exact prediction (squared bias computed from the neighbour sets, plus
\(\sigma^2/k\)) and the average over 400 simulated data sets agree at every \(k\), within 3% at \(k=1\)
and better elsewhere. The squared bias is negligible until the neighbourhood, \(k/100\) of the
interval, reaches a noticeable part of a period of the sine. Beyond that it grows quickly:
from 0.0004 at \(k=12\) to 0.030 at \(k=40\). The best \(k\) is 18, with error 0.0067, against 0.090
for \(k=1\).

> Iris and wine

Fisher's iris data have 150 flowers of three species with four measurements in centimetres.
The wine data have 178 wines from three cultivars with 13 chemical measurements in very
different units. Proline, for example, runs from 278 to 1680, while hue runs from 0.48 to 1.71.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/knn_iris.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::size_t K = 50;
  const dm::stats::KNNClassifier petals(iris, "Species ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width", {.k = 1});
  const dm::stats::KNNClassifier all4(iris, "Species ~ .", {.k = 1});
  const auto path_petals = petals.loo_accuracy_path(K), path_all = all4.loo_accuracy_path(K);
  auto best = [](const std::vector<double>& p) {
    const auto it = std::max_element(p.begin(), p.end());
    return std::pair{static_cast<std::size_t>(it - p.begin()) + 1, *it};
  };
  const auto [kp, ap] = best(path_petals);
  const auto [ka, aa] = best(path_all);
  std::print("Best leave-one-out accuracy: petals {:.4f} at k = {}, all four {:.4f} at k = {}\n\n", ap, kp, aa, ka);
  DF iris_table;
  iris_table.add_column("k", std::vector<double>{1, 5, 10, 15, 25, 50});
  std::vector<double> col_p, col_a;
  for (const std::size_t k : {1u, 5u, 10u, 15u, 25u, 50u}) col_p.push_back(path_petals[k - 1]), col_a.push_back(path_all[k - 1]);
  iris_table.add_column("LOO accuracy, petals", col_p);
  iris_table.add_column("LOO accuracy, all four", col_a);
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  md.column_digits = {{"k", 0}};
  std::print("{}\n\n", iris_table.to_md(md));

  const dm::stats::KNNClassifier chosen(iris, "Species ~ Petal.Length + Petal.Width", {.k = 15});
  const auto cm = chosen.confusion_matrix();
  DF confusion;
  confusion.add_column("observed", chosen.classes());
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < 3; ++c) confusion.add_column(chosen.classes()[c], std::vector<double>{cm(0, c), cm(1, c), cm(2, c)});
  std::print("Leave-one-out confusion matrix, petals, k = 15 (columns: predicted):\n\n{}\n\n", confusion.to_md(0));

  // Wine: standardised against raw predictors.
  const auto wine = dm::datasets::load("wine");
  const dm::stats::KNNClassifier std_wine(wine, "cultivar ~ .", {.k = 1});
  const dm::stats::KNNClassifier raw_wine(wine, "cultivar ~ .", {.k = 1, .standardize = false});
  const dm::stats::KNNClassifier proline(wine, "cultivar ~ proline", {.k = 1});
  const auto ps = std_wine.loo_accuracy_path(25), pr = raw_wine.loo_accuracy_path(25), pp = proline.loo_accuracy_path(25);
  DF wine_table;
  wine_table.add_column("k", std::vector<double>{1, 5, 15, 25});
  std::vector<double> s, r, o;
  for (const std::size_t k : {1u, 5u, 15u, 25u}) s.push_back(ps[k - 1]), r.push_back(pr[k - 1]), o.push_back(pp[k - 1]);
  wine_table.add_column("standardised, 13 predictors", s);
  wine_table.add_column("raw, 13 predictors", r);
  wine_table.add_column("proline alone", o);
  std::print("Wine, leave-one-out accuracy:\n\n{}\n", wine_table.to_md(md));

  auto paths = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> kk;
  for (std::size_t k = 1; k <= K; ++k) kk.push_back(double(k));
  paths.lines(kk, path_petals, "petals", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0).lines(kk, path_all, "all four", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0);
  std::print("Lowest accuracy on the path: {:.4f}\n\n", std::min(std::ranges::min(path_petals), std::ranges::min(path_all)));
  paths.x_limits(0.0, 51.0).y_limits(0.84, 1.0).title("Iris: leave-one-out accuracy").x_label("k").y_label("accuracy");

  auto regions = chosen.plot_decision_regions("Petal.Length", "Petal.Width", 200, std::array{0.7, 7.2, -0.15, 3.3});
  std::vector<double> mx, my;
  const auto& pl = iris.get_column<double>("Petal.Length").values();
  const auto& pw = iris.get_column<double>("Petal.Width").values();
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < pl.size(); ++i)
    if (chosen.fitted_classes()[i] != chosen.training_labels()[i]) mx.push_back(pl[i]), my.push_back(pw[i]);
  regions.points(mx, my, "LOO error", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 3.0);
  regions.title("Petal regions, k = 15");

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(paths).add(regions).size(1100, 480).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/knn_iris.svg");
}
```
