Lowest accuracy on the path: 0.8733

\image(figures/knn_iris.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: leave-one-out accuracy on iris against \(k\), using the two petal measurements (blue) or all four (red). Right: the 15-NN decision regions in the petal plane, with the training points coloured by species and the leave-one-out errors marked by a black centre. Tables: accuracies at selected \(k\); the leave-one-out confusion matrix of the petal model with \(k=15\); and leave-one-out accuracy on wine with and without standardisation.)
\alttext(Left: leave-one-out accuracy against k from 1 to 50. The blue petal curve is flat at 0.96 for most k, with 0.967 at k equals 4, 5 and 7 and brief dips to 0.947. The red curve for all four measurements is between 0.94 and 0.967 up to about k equals 35, then falls to 0.873 at k equals 46 and 48 to 50. Right: the petal plane shaded blue at the lower left, pink in a diagonal band and green at the upper right, with blue setosa points in a tight cluster, red versicolor points in the middle, green virginica points at the upper right, and black centres on six points near the versicolor–virginica border.)

*Iris.* Setosa is separated from the other species by a wide margin, so the whole difficulty
is the versicolor–virginica border, and the petal measurements carry nearly all the
information. With the petals alone the LOO accuracy is 96–96.7% for almost every \(k\) up to 50.
With all four measurements the sepals add noise to the distances: the accuracy is lower for
small \(k\) and falls to 87.3% by \(k=46\), where the neighbourhoods are almost a third of a class. The
best \(k\) for all four (96.7% at \(k=13\) to 18) only matches the petals. Choosing \(k\) by maximising
the LOO accuracy is itself optimistic, since the maximum of 50 noisy estimates is biased
upwards; a gap of one flower (0.67%) is noise. With \(k=15\) on the petals, all six LOO errors are
on the versicolor–virginica border (two versicolor called virginica, four the other way round),
as the region plot shows. The four-measurement path for \(k=1,\dots,25\) was checked against scikit-learn's
`KNeighborsClassifier` refitted with each row left out (and its `StandardScaler` refitted too):
all 25 values agree exactly.

*Wine.* Without standardisation the distances are dominated by proline, whose standard deviation
(314) is 22 times that of magnesium, the next largest, and over 90 times that of any other
predictor. The 13-predictor accuracy (70–77%) is
then close to that of proline alone (66–70%). Standardised, the same 13 predictors reach
95–98%. Here the fix is free because every predictor carries some information. With noise
predictors, as above, standardisation would give the noise full weight.

> Conclusion

The \(k\)-nearest-neighbour rule needs no model, only a distance, and the theory says what that
buys. With \(k\) fixed, the large-sample error is a known function of \(P(Y=1\mid x)\): at most twice the
Bayes risk for \(k=1\), and close to it for large \(k\). In regression the error is exactly the
squared bias of averaging \(f\) over the neighbourhood plus \(\sigma^2/k\). Both were reproduced here by
simulation. The cost is that "near" must mean something. Predictors must be on comparable
scales: on wine, standardising lifted the accuracy from about 72% to 97%. Uninformative
predictors must be left out: 128 noise predictors pushed the error from 0.165 to 0.381 as distances
concentrated. Leave-one-out evaluation is the honest error estimate for k-NN, and datamunge
computes it exactly, rescaling for every left-out row, and for all \(k\) at once from one sort per
row. On iris it agrees with scikit-learn to the last flower.

